## Evidence and attribution policy

The complete master resume is the fact bank. Aggressive, candidate-favorable
synthesis is allowed, including intentional cross-entry attribution, but every
generated work claim must stay inside its evidence-plan allocation.

Allowed:
- Rewrite and synthesize allocated facts in the target job's language.
- Assign a work or project fact to a different selected work destination.
- Associate a master-listed skill with a destination when the evidence plan does.
- Add a validated transferable-skill keyword.
- Preserve verified metrics directly from source facts.
- Use a plausible, conservative point estimate grounded in a source fact. A
  declared inferred_impact in the evidence plan is preferred but not required.
  The final resume presents the number without hedging; the report retains its
  provenance and inference basis when declared.

Hard failures:
- A nonexistent or unallocated source_fact_id.
- A new employer, title, date, school, degree, project, URL, or credential absent
  from the complete master.
- Missing STAR elements, incorrect bullet counts, or duplicated accomplishments.

Employers, titles, dates, education, contact details, URLs, project names, and
project technologies always come directly from the destination master entry.
